    <h2>Part 1: Musicological DSP Rules &amp; AI Knowledge Base</h2>

    <h3>Subgenre &amp; Vocal Chain Specifications</h3>

    <p><strong>UK / NY / DE Drill (Luciano, Pop Smoke):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Metric:</em> 140–145 BPM, syncopated off-beat snares, gliding 808s</li>
      <li>
        <em>Vocal Chain:</em> Dry/Direct (close-miking). High-Pass strict 110 Hz (against 808 mud). Presence
        Boost +3 dB @ 1.5–3 kHz, highs rounded above 10 kHz. Aggressive FET Compression (Ratio 6:1–8:1,
        Fast Attack 5 ms, 6–10 dB GR, R-Vox-Style)
      </li>
      <li><em>Space:</em> Extremely dry Short-Room-Reverb (&lt; 0.8s Decay, &lt; 10% Wet)</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Cloud Rap (PNL, RIN, Yung Hurn):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Metric:</em> 110–130 BPM, Lo-Fi textures, Staccato/Legato switching</li>
      <li>
        <em>Vocal Chain:</em> Hard Autotune (Retune 0–3 ms, Humanize 0). High-Shelf Air-EQ (+4 dB @ 12
        kHz). Spectral resonance suppression (Soothe2-logic) for sibilance. Multiband Opto-Compression
        (CL1B-Style) for warmth
      </li>
      <li>
        <em>Space:</em> Shimmer Reverb (Pitch-Shift +12 semitones, 4–8s Decay). Haas effect (&gt; 25ms
        Delay for extreme stereo width)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Dance-Rap / Pop-Hop (Ski Aggu, Miksu/Macloud):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Metric:</em> 120–135 BPM, 4-to-the-floor kicks</li>
      <li>
        <em>Vocal Chain:</em> Radical Mud-Cut (200–400 Hz, −4 dB). Transparent pop highs (3–5 kHz boost)
      </li>
      <li>
        <em>Space:</em> Synchronized Ping-Pong Delays (1/8 or 1/16 Dotted) with Phaser modulation for
        kinetic energy
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Frequency Hierarchy &amp; Low-End Management</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Kick vs. 808:</strong> Kick triggers Sidechain-Ducking that drops 808 at 50–80 Hz by 3–6
        dB. Soft-Clipping/Saturation for mobile speaker playback
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Flow Adaptation:</strong> Sequencer grid detects triplet flows and adapts hi-hat density
        adaptively (e.g. from 1/16 to 1/32 triplets)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Part 2: C++ Native Engine &amp; Latency Optimization</h2>

    <h3>AAudio &amp; Threading Rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>API Configuration:</strong>
        <code>PerformanceMode::LowLatency</code> + <code>SharingMode::Exclusive</code> (Oboe/AAudio)
      </li>
      <li><strong>Buffer Size:</strong> Dynamic via <code>framesPerBurst</code></li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zero-Allocation Rule:</strong>
        <code>new</code>, <code>malloc</code>, <code>push_back</code>, <code>std::mutex</code> strictly
        forbidden in <code>dataCallback</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lock-Free Parameters:</strong>
        <code>std::atomic&lt;float&gt;</code> with <code>std::memory_order_relaxed</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>CPU Load Shedding:</strong> At &gt; 92% utilization, dynamically disables expensive effects
        (Graceful Degradation)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>GhostHand Live Beat-Carving (C++ Backend)</h3>
    <pre><code>// Lock-free Dynamic Ducking based on Vocal RMS
struct BeatCarverParams &#123;
    std::atomic&lt;float&gt; vocalRmsEnergy&#123;0.0f&#125;;
    std::atomic&lt;float&gt; beatCarveDepthDb&#123;0.0f&#125;;
&#125;;
// Inside Audio Callback:
float rms = calculateRMS(vocalBuffer, numFrames);
if (rms &gt; 0.05f) &#123;
    float targetDucking = -4.5f * (rms / 0.5f);
    params_.beatCarveDepthDb.store(
        std::max(-6.0f, targetDucking),
        std::memory_order_relaxed);
&#125;</code></pre>

